                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><m>n+5</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>n</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>n-5</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><m>n-30</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>n-35</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>n-40</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </p>
        </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</page>
<page>
<exercise>
        <p>
            Generalize the solution to this polygon puzzle by writing the upper left corner as <m>p</m>
            and then writing the remaining vertices in terms of <m>p</m>.
        </p>
        <image source="puzzle1.png" width="35%">
            <shortdescription>Square polygon puzzle with sides 8, 14, 12, and 6 moving clockwise around the square. </shortdescription>
        </image>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To find the upper right vertex we subtract <m>p</m> from 8.  
                To find the lower left vertex we subtract <m>p</m> from 6.
                To find the lower right vertex we can subtract <m>8-p</m> from 14.
                <md>
                    14-(8-p)
                </md>
                <md>
                    14-8-(-p)
                </md>
                <md>
                    6+p
                </md>
                To find the lower right vertex we could also have subtracted <m>6-p</m> from 12.
                <md>12-(6-p)</md>
                <md>12-6-(-p)</md>
                <md>6+p</md>
            </p>
            <image source="puzzle1s.png" width="35%">
            <shortdescription>Square polygon puzzle with sides 8, 14, 12, and 6 moving clockwise around the square. </shortdescription>
        </image>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
</page>
<page>

    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
    <p>
        Simplify the following expression using correct order of operations:
        <md>
            \frac{5-7}{8-4}(3+5)-4\div4\cdot3+2^3\cdot2
        </md>
        
    </p>
    <solution>
        <p>
             <md>
            \frac{5-7}{8-4}(3+5)-4\div4\cdot3+2^3\cdot2
            </md>
            <md>
            \frac{-2}{8-4}(3+5)-4\div4\cdot3+2^3\cdot2
            </md>
            <md>
            \frac{-2}{4}(3+5)-4\div4\cdot3+2^3\cdot2
            </md>
            <md>
            \frac{-2}{4}\cdot8-4\div4\cdot3+2^3\cdot2
            </md>
            <md>
            \frac{-2}{4}\cdot8-4\div4\cdot3+8\cdot2
            </md>
            <md>
            -\frac{1}{2}\cdot8-4\div4\cdot3+8\cdot2
            </md>
            <md>
            -4-4\div4\cdot3+8\cdot2
            </md>
            <md>
            -4-1\cdot3+8\cdot2
            </md>
            <md>
            -4-3+8\cdot2
            </md>
            <md>
            -4-3+16
            </md>
            <md>
            -7+16
            </md>
            <md>
            9
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise workspace="0.5in">
    <p>
        Simplify the following expression using correct order of operations:
        <md>
            4\div2^2-5\cdot(9-7)+15\div3\cdot2^3
        </md>
        
    </p>
    <solution>
        <p>
             <md>
            4\div2^2-5\cdot(9-7)+15\div3\cdot2^3
            </md>
            <md>
            4\div2^2-5\cdot2+15\div3\cdot2^3
            </md>
            <md>
            4\div4-5\cdot2+15\div3\cdot2^3
            </md>
            <md>
            4\div4-5\cdot2+15\div3\cdot8
            </md>
            <md>
            1-5\cdot2+15\div3\cdot8
            </md>
            <md>
            1-10+15\div3\cdot8
            </md>
            <md>
            1-10+5\cdot8
            </md>
            <md>
            1-10+40
            </md>
            <md>
            -9+40
            </md>
            <md>
            31
            </md>
            
        </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</page>
</worksheet>


